    "body": "- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\r\n- [x] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\r\n\r\nElixir files come under the extensions `.ex` and `.exs` (among others, but these are working properly).\r\n\r\nIn `mini.icons`, there is an explicit mapping for `.exs` files, even though:\r\n- It's not the most common extension you'll come across, by far\r\n- It already works well with `vim.filetype.match()`\r\n\r\nHowever, `.ex` has no explicit mapping, even though:\r\n- It is the most common elixir file extension you'll see\r\n- It currently doesn't work with `vim.filetype.match()` for some reason\r\n\r\nThis PR adjusts the mapping so that both extensions are represented in their own specific ways (there is a nerd symbol for elixir scripts that's distinct from normal elixir files so I made use of that). If the mapping for `.exs` sounds too specific for the scope of this module, I guess it would also be fine to remove it entirely.",
    "closed_at": "2024-07-09T11:03:25Z",
    "comment_data": [],
    "comment_regular_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the PR!\r\n\r\nThe 'exs' extension was added as built-in extension, because it is the one which [Neovim's default `vim.filetype.match()` recognizes unconditionally](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/blob/b3d94b10870ce494941279f78387e1d98a7e48e7/runtime/lua/vim/filetype.lua#L395).\r\n\r\nThe 'ex' extensions was not added because [it is not recognized unconditionally](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/blob/b3d94b10870ce494941279f78387e1d98a7e48e7/runtime/lua/vim/filetype.lua#L419). In particular, it [can be matched as Euphoria language](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/blob/b3d94b10870ce494941279f78387e1d98a7e48e7/runtime/lua/vim/filetype/detect.lua#L556-L557) (which indeed [also uses '.ex' extension](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euphoria_(programming_language))).\r\n\r\n> * It currently doesn't work with `vim.filetype.match()` for some reason\r\n\r\nI am not sure why, because for me it does. Executing `:=MiniIcons.get('file', 'hello.ex')` returns icon data for 'elixir' filetype.\r\n\r\n> This PR adjusts the mapping so that both extensions are represented in their own specific ways (there is a nerd symbol for elixir scripts that's distinct from normal elixir files so I made use of that). If the mapping for `.exs` sounds too specific for the scope of this module, I guess it would also be fine to remove it entirely.\r\n\r\nI am happy to add 'elixirscript' as separate filetype if there is evidence it is a relatively used one.",
