        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "Thanks for the quick reply!\r\n\r\n> The 'ex' extensions was not added because [it is not recognized unconditionally](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/blob/b3d94b10870ce494941279f78387e1d98a7e48e7/runtime/lua/vim/filetype.lua#L419). In particular, it [can be matched as Euphoria language](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/blob/b3d94b10870ce494941279f78387e1d98a7e48e7/runtime/lua/vim/filetype/detect.lua#L556-L557) (which indeed [also uses '.ex' extension](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euphoria_(programming_language))).\r\n\r\nThanks for the insight, I had no idea about euphoria, and yeah I guess it makes sense.\r\n\r\n> I am not sure why, because for me it does. Executing :=MiniIcons.get('file', 'hello.ex') returns icon data for 'elixir' filetype.\r\n\r\nHuh. Maybe there's something going on on my side then, I'll look into that.\r\n\r\n> I am happy to add 'elixirscript' as separate filetype if there is evidence it is a relatively used one.\r\n\r\nWell, it *is* commonly used if only because a typical elixir package will always come with a `mix.exs` script as the main setup utility. It's also the format used to write tests and migrations, so you'll see a lot of those in a big project.\r\n\r\nHowever, it's not an actual `filetype` as far as neovim is concerned (it's still only recognized as `elixir`, which makes sense since it's the same thing within). But there *is* a dedicated icon for those so maybe we still want to make use of it? I guess it would be similar to `.h` files that are still `C` code, but come with their own mappings.",
